			<ns0:title type="html">George A. Romero’s Season of the Witch is a slow-burn feminist occult drama</ns0:title>
			<ns0:link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/1009346/george-a-romero-season-of-the-witch-feminist-horror-review" />
			<ns0:id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=1009346</ns0:id>
			<ns0:updated>2026-10-11T11:12:52-04:00</ns0:updated>
			<ns0:published>2026-10-11T11:15:00-04:00</ns0:published>
			<ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Column" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Entertainment" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Film" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Movie Review" />
							<ns0:summary type="html">George A. Romero is obviously best known as the father of the modern zombie movie. But between inventing the genre with Night of the Living Dead and his outbreak horror, The Crazies, Romero made two much lesser-known films: There's Always Vanilla, which I'll admit I've never seen, and Season of the </ns0:summary>
			
