			<ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Culture" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Entertainment" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Film" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Internet Culture" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Interview" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Report" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Streaming" /><ns0:category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="TV Shows" />
							<ns0:summary type="html">From Disney Plus to HBO Max, almost every major established streaming service is experimenting with ways to get into vertical content. It's clear that these companies see video-focused social media platforms like TikTok and Instagram as existential threats. In this arms race for viewers' attention, </ns0:summary>
			
